The scenario becomes a corpus, and the forest runs

Dev, on the previous state of this work: "You are not generating any source code because you are not
parsing the drake into json. Also, you are writing Scala value expressions instead of using drake
expression syntax that should be parsed into json format expression tree." Both were true, and acting
on the second is what found the two defects below.

NOTHING WAS BEING GENERATED

`src/test/resources/scenario` held 23 `.drake` and nothing else. The gate parsed drake in memory,
wrote JSON to a scratch classpath root purely so `TypeLoader` could resolve names, compiled to a
scratch directory, and deleted both. Nothing outlived the test, which means nothing was generated.
A trio with two thirds manufactured in temp space is a fixture, not a corpus.

The scenario is now a full trio — `X.drake` beside `X.json` under `src/test/resources/scenario`, and
the projection committed under `src/test/scala/scenario`. Every artifact's path comes from the type's
own `TypeName`, so the three agree by construction rather than by convention. Generation runs the real
route: parse each drake to its normative JSON, then generate each `.scala` from the JSON as LOADED
through `TypeLoader` — not from the in-memory parse — so what is projected is what the normative
artifact says.

The gate got SMALLER as a result, which is the argument for doing it. Because the projected Scala is a
real source tree, sbt compiles it and compilation needs no gate of its own — the same transitive
argument `DracoGenTest` rests on. Because the scenario types are on the classpath like any others, the
execution gate calls them directly: the reflection, the child classloader and the private
`KnowledgeService` all went away. Four gates now, and they ASSERT rather than report:

    1  PROJECTS      generate(X.json) == the committed X.scala      (DracoGenTest's gate)
    2  MEANS IT      rules an actor OWNS vs rules it ACCEPTS        1 of 1
    3  IS CONSISTENT every transform member is one leaf conversion  2 of 2
    4  RUNS          0.8 uM -> 0.496 uM across the boundary         1 of 2 alarms crossed

Gate 1 was verified by breaking it (rename a `Null` in a projected source; it names the type and the
path). Gate 3 likewise, by pointing `Potency` at the source side instead of the target.

DRAKE EXPRESSIONS, AND THE TWO DEFECTS THEY EXPOSED

The receiving rule had been authored as raw Scala. On drake's application surface it parses to a real
tree —

    loc received java.util.List(scenario.birch.BirchJasmonate) ctx.getRuntime parameters
      .get parameters par "received"
    mon received.add parameters par jasmonate

    {"()":[{".":[{"()":["ctx.getRuntime"]},"get"]},"\"received\""]}

— and doing that broke two things that writing Scala had been hiding:

**`Drake.emit` crashed.** `unfoldChain` did `empty.head` on a chain whose base receiver is a NILADIC
call (`ctx.getRuntime()`): it assumed the receiver-application's function is a `.`-path, and a plain
glued token has no path elements. Such an application IS the chain's base, and `valueLines` now writes
it with its own `parameters`, empty list included, exactly as a chain call does.

**`Generator.actionBody` had no `Local` case.** A `loc` in a RULE action fell through to the
BodyElement catch-all: the initializer emitted as a bare statement, the binding dropped, every later
reference to the name left dangling. `factoryBody` and `actorActionBody` both carry the case; this
site was missed when `Local` was added as the eleventh element kind.

Neither was reachable while the corpus wrote Scala into its value slots. That is the argument for the
rule, stated better than an argument could: host-opaque text is not a shortcut, it is where the bugs
go to hide.

A census then converted what drake can already say. Seven host-opaque values became four:
`knowledge.newStatefulSession()`, `session.fire()` and `ctx.insert(primed)` are applications and now
parse to trees. The four that remain cannot be said yet and are not stubbornness — `msg match {…}`
wants #58, and `_compound.value`, `_potency.value * 0.62` and `jasmonate.potency.value > 0.5` are
paths and infix operators, which is #61.

`super` PROJECTS

`actorKnowledge` read the actor's own domain and stopped, so a transform rule owned by the root that
spans both species reached neither side's actor. It compiled, ran, and matched nothing, with no error
anywhere to report it. It now walks the `superDomain` chain, cycle-guarded because a super edge is
authored and an authored edge can be wrong, spelling each rule package-relative:

    private lazy val knowledge: Knowledge = {
      val k = Rule.knowledgeService.newKnowledge("RootInterface")
      scenario.forest.AshBirchAlarm.ruleType.pattern.accept(k)
      k
    }

That is also what the fact-resolution constraint needs: rules split across sessions cannot match
across the split, and co-residency in ONE knowledge is what a root super-domain owning the transform
rules buys.

Gate 2 was rewritten in the same breath, and the reason matters more than the change. It had been
counting what the DEFINITIONS own, so it described the gap rather than detecting it, and would have
gone on reporting `UNREACHABLE=1` after the fix landed. It counts `.ruleType.pattern.accept` in the
PROJECTED SOURCE now — the projection, not the definition.

WHAT GATE 3 DELIBERATELY DOES NOT REQUIRE

That a transform member derive the transform domain. `Potency` IS a birch `Micromolar` and LIVES IN
`Ash_Birch`; containment is the domain pointer, not an inheritance edge. Everywhere else in every
corpus the two coincide — every domain member derives its domain — and this is the first place they
genuinely come apart. A property of transform domains worth stating, not a hole worth filling.

IT RUNS

`BirchAlarmReceived` joins `Forest`, and it is not decoration: Evrete gives an RHS-inserted fact no
working-memory node unless some rule consumes it, so without a receiving end `ctx.insert(primed)` had
nowhere to land. Biologically it is the half the scenario was missing — the birch side taking
delivery.

    crossed: potency=0.496 uM  compound=jasmonic-acid

Two alarms in, one out: 0.8 uM crosses, 0.3 uM does not. The threshold is in `AshBirchAlarm`'s
condition and the 0.62 factor lives nowhere but `Potency`'s factory body, so both numbers being right
is the evidence that the guard and the formula survived projection.

Not exercised, and stated plainly: the actor MEMBRANE. `RootInterface.receive` is still the Scala
`match` in the list above; running it would prove nothing about any other target.
